; expect-fail: wasm — codegen does not support a bare `None` symbol (emits
; `unbound symbol 'None'`), so this program can't compile to wasm at all. That
; is an acceptable gap: wasm not compiling is fine, silently getting the
; truthiness of None WRONG would not be.
;
; The ruling under test: the falsy set is exactly {false, (), None} — a value
; is truthy unless it says no (false) or says nothing ((), None). None drives
; the ELSE branch; Some(x) is truthy for ANY x, including Some(false) and
; Some(()), because the Option wrapper "says something" regardless of payload.
; The EIR VM and the legacy interpreter agree.
[fn main []
  [println [if None "TRUTHY" "none-falsy"]]
  [println [if [Some false] "some-false-truthy" "FALSY"]]
  [println [if [Some [do]] "some-unit-truthy" "FALSY"]]
  [println [if [Some 0] "some-zero-truthy" "FALSY"]]
  [println [or None "default-via-or"]]]
